title: MikroTik REST API: Network Interfaces
agents: mikrotik_rest_api
catalog: hw/network/mikrotik
license: GPL
distribution: check_mk
description:
 Traffic, packets, errors, discards, operational state and speed of the
 network interfaces of a MikroTik RouterOS device, read via the RouterOS
 REST API ({/interface}).

 The data is handed to the standard Checkmk interface check, so all its
 rules apply: {Network interface and switch port discovery} for the
 discovery and {Network interfaces and switch ports} for the levels
 (bandwidth, error and discard rates, expected speed and state, averaging,
 interface groups).

 The RouterOS interface type is mapped to the IANA ifType (ether 6,
 vlan 135, bridge 209, bond 161, wlan/wifi 71, PPP 23, tunnels 131,
 vrrp 112, WireGuard, loopback and others 1). ether, vlan, bridge,
 WireGuard and loopback match the ifType RouterOS reports via SNMP. With the default
 discovery rule only ethernet ports that are up are discovered, the same
 as for SNMP. Add port types to the discovery rule to monitor VLANs,
 bridges, bondings, PPP or tunnel interfaces.

 The speed is the negotiated rate of ethernet and SFP ports
 ({/interface/ethernet/monitor}); other interface types report no speed.
 Disabled interfaces are reported as admin down.

 Index, description and alias are the same as via SNMP: the index is
 the RouterOS internal id (ifIndex), which does not change when other
 interfaces are added or removed, the description is the interface name
 (ifDescr), the alias is the interface comment and empty without one
 (ifAlias). Switching a host from SNMP to this agent keeps the items.

 To connect to the MikroTik device a special datasource program is
 used: {MikroTik RouterOS (mikrotik-rest-api)} in Setup > Agents > Other integrations,
 module "Network interfaces".

 Added in mikrotik-rest-api 1.0.0 by Klaus Paulitsch.

item:
 Depending on the discovery rule the index (default), the name
 (description) or the comment (alias) of the interface.

perfdata:
 Same as the standard interface check: in/out bandwidth, packets,
 errors and discards.

inventory:
 One service per interface that matches the discovery rule.
